\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{22}{section*.16}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces The light blue and light red lines show the design sensitivity of the aLIGO and LIGO A+ detectors. The quantum noise, noise due to radiation pressure and photon shot noise, is shown in dark-blue (aLIGO) and dark-red (A+). Above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, quantum effects are one of the limiting sources of noise. Below 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, classical noise sources, e.g.\nobreakspace  {}seismic motion, limit the sensitivity of the detector. Above 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, shot noise limits the sensitivity of both detectors. Shot noise rises with $f$ due to the arm cavities. Below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the quantum noise goes as $1/f$ due to radiation pressure. Coating thermal noise will limit the A+ detectors's sensitivity around $\sim 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\hertz }$.\relax }}{23}{figure.caption.17}}
\newlabel{fig:detectorchapterdesignsensitivitycurves}{{2.3}{23}{The light blue and light red lines show the design sensitivity of the aLIGO and LIGO A+ detectors. The quantum noise, noise due to radiation pressure and photon shot noise, is shown in dark-blue (aLIGO) and dark-red (A+). Above 10\,Hz, quantum effects are one of the limiting sources of noise. Below 10\,Hz, classical noise sources, e.g.~seismic motion, limit the sensitivity of the detector. Above 100\,Hz, shot noise limits the sensitivity of both detectors. Shot noise rises with $f$ due to the arm cavities. Below 100\,Hz, the quantum noise goes as $1/f$ due to radiation pressure. Coating thermal noise will limit the A+ detectors's sensitivity around $\sim 100\,\si {\hertz }$.\relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
