\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces The top panel: as the second amplifier head's temperature increased, the power produced by it decreased. The bottom panel: The higher order mode content increased as the temperature of the amplifier increased. This means that the power in the fundamental mode decreased as the temperature decreased due to two effects: less overall power and worse beam quality.\relax }}{57}{figure.caption.63}}
\newlabel{fig:ligo_laser_chapter_cooling_pwoer_hom}{{3.9}{57}{The top panel: as the second amplifier head's temperature increased, the power produced by it decreased. The bottom panel: The higher order mode content increased as the temperature of the amplifier increased. This means that the power in the fundamental mode decreased as the temperature decreased due to two effects: less overall power and worse beam quality.\relax }{figure.caption.63}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Mode scans were performed with the DBB for each amplifier head temperature. The DBB's auto-alignment system minimised the power in the first order mode. The second order mode for this cavity is located at $\sim 0.3$ FSR; its exact location drifts due to thermal expansion of the cavity. There is some power in the fourth order mode. As the temperature of the amplifier head changes, the second order mode content of the beam changes at the percent level. This second order mode height is shown in the inset. The second order mode changes due to the beam's astigmatism changing.\relax }}{58}{figure.caption.64}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptermodescans}{{3.10}{58}{Mode scans were performed with the DBB for each amplifier head temperature. The DBB's auto-alignment system minimised the power in the first order mode. The second order mode for this cavity is located at $\sim 0.3$ FSR; its exact location drifts due to thermal expansion of the cavity. There is some power in the fourth order mode. As the temperature of the amplifier head changes, the second order mode content of the beam changes at the percent level. This second order mode height is shown in the inset. The second order mode changes due to the beam's astigmatism changing.\relax }{figure.caption.64}{}}
