\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5f}{{6.1}{153}{The LO beam enters HAM6 directly above BHDBS2 (north side), and it is highlighted in green. The signal beam enters HAM6 directly above OM0 (north side), and it is highlighted in pink. The signal beam is steered by OM0 onto BHDBS2 where it is combined with the LO. The two combined beams are shown in red. These are incident upon a mirror used for steering (OMxS), and then upon two mirrors curved mirrors (OMx1 and OMx2). The beams enter the OMCs, which are seismically isolated by the BHSS (the large triangular component). The spacings for these components are given in Table~\ref {tab:layout_parameters}.\relax }{figure.caption.191}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Astigmatism is a source of loss. When designing the layout shown in Figure \ref  {fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5f}, the AOI for the curved mirrors was altered to explore how sensitive it was to astigmatism. Using Equation (\ref  {eq:bhd_for_ligo_mm_loss}), the loss was computed. This effect is negligible up to 10\si {\degree } after which it starts to become significant compared to the losses summarised in Table \ref  {tab:HAM6_losses}.\relax }}{154}{figure.caption.196}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterastigmatism}{{6.2}{154}{Astigmatism is a source of loss. When designing the layout shown in Figure \ref {fig:bhdforligochapterlayout5f}, the AOI for the curved mirrors was altered to explore how sensitive it was to astigmatism. Using Equation (\ref {eq:bhd_for_ligo_mm_loss}), the loss was computed. This effect is negligible up to 10\si {\degree } after which it starts to become significant compared to the losses summarised in Table \ref {tab:HAM6_losses}.\relax }{figure.caption.196}{}}
