\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterbeamshape}{{6.3}{155}{The top panel shows the radius of the beam as it travels from the SRM (z = 0) to the waist of the OMC. Significant points along the axis are marked with vertical lines. Less significant, but useful, reference points are marked by dashed vertical lines. The bottom panel shows the Gouy phase shift accumulated by the beam as it propagates along the optical axis.\relax }{figure.caption.198}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Top panel: The choice of OFI lens power affects the combination of OMx1 and OMx2 power that is required for 100\% mode-matching. Bottom panel: The best choice for of OFI lens that corresponds to obtaining orthogonal control of the beam parameter in HAM6 is where the Gouy phase separation between the pair of OMx1 and OMx2 is closest to \ang {45}. The most Gouy phase separation between OMx1 and OMx2 achievable by this layout is \ang {42}.\relax }}{156}{figure.caption.199}}
\newlabel{fig:bhdforligochapterlenschoice}{{6.4}{156}{Top panel: The choice of OFI lens power affects the combination of OMx1 and OMx2 power that is required for 100\% mode-matching. Bottom panel: The best choice for of OFI lens that corresponds to obtaining orthogonal control of the beam parameter in HAM6 is where the Gouy phase separation between the pair of OMx1 and OMx2 is closest to \ang {45}. The most Gouy phase separation between OMx1 and OMx2 achievable by this layout is \ang {42}.\relax }{figure.caption.199}{}}
