\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Optical layout used for measuring the effect that increasing the reverse bias of a photodiode had on its quantum efficiency and saturation limit. The out-of-loop photodiode, $\rm  {PD_{OOL}}$, was the photodiode under test (FD10D). A 1.55\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \metre } laser was used to assess the optical properties of the photodiode. A combination of half wave plate (HWP) and polarised beam splitter (PBS) allowed for the power of the beam to be set and a lens was used to set the size of the beam on the photodiode under test. Amplitude stabilised light was needed to ensure the increase in photocurrent seen when the bias increased was due to an increase in quantum efficiency. The photodiode circuit (TIA) (see Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:circuits_appendix_variable_bias_ad797}) allowed for shot noise limited measurements of current around 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA and for the bias to be selected.\relax }}{206}{figure.caption.253}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchaptershotnoisetestsetup}{{8.1}{206}{Optical layout used for measuring the effect that increasing the reverse bias of a photodiode had on its quantum efficiency and saturation limit. The out-of-loop photodiode, $\rm {PD_{OOL}}$, was the photodiode under test (FD10D). A 1.55\,\si {\micro \metre } laser was used to assess the optical properties of the photodiode. A combination of half wave plate (HWP) and polarised beam splitter (PBS) allowed for the power of the beam to be set and a lens was used to set the size of the beam on the photodiode under test. Amplitude stabilised light was needed to ensure the increase in photocurrent seen when the bias increased was due to an increase in quantum efficiency. The photodiode circuit (TIA) (see Appendix~\ref {sec:circuits_appendix_variable_bias_ad797}) allowed for shot noise limited measurements of current around 10\,mA and for the bias to be selected.\relax }{figure.caption.253}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.4.2}Results}{207}{subsection.8.4.2}}
\newlabel{sec:pd_chapter_qe_results}{{8.4.2}{207}{Results}{subsection.8.4.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces The FD10D's quantum efficiency depends on its reverse bias; the increase in quantum efficiency was $15\pm 1\%$ when the bias was increased to the maximum value specified in its datasheet. Two types of measurement were used to determine the increase in quantum efficiency as the bias was increased. The first method was to measure the DC response, and the corresponding results are shown as circles. The second method involved modulating the bias and measuring the resulting modulation in the photocurrent, i.e.\nobreakspace  {}an AC measurement, and these are shown by the stars. Each set of measurements was done with a different intensity by changing either the beam's width or power. Further detail on both methods is given in Section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:pd_chapter_qe_method}.\relax }}{208}{figure.caption.254}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10dqeasfnofbias}{{8.2}{208}{The FD10D's quantum efficiency depends on its reverse bias; the increase in quantum efficiency was $15\pm 1\%$ when the bias was increased to the maximum value specified in its datasheet. Two types of measurement were used to determine the increase in quantum efficiency as the bias was increased. The first method was to measure the DC response, and the corresponding results are shown as circles. The second method involved modulating the bias and measuring the resulting modulation in the photocurrent, i.e.~an AC measurement, and these are shown by the stars. Each set of measurements was done with a different intensity by changing either the beam's width or power. Further detail on both methods is given in Section~\ref {sec:pd_chapter_qe_method}.\relax }{figure.caption.254}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, and it was exposed to 15\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mW of 1.55\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \metre } light. 15\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA of photocurrent was generated. The noise of the photocurrent produced by the FD10D (blue line) corresponds to the level predicted by Equation\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} (orange line) meaning that the quantum efficiency of the FD10D did increase rather than the increase in photocurrent being the result of an avalanche process. The 1111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz signal was used to calibrate the measurement as this modulation also could be measured on the high-performance in-loop photodiode. \relax }}{210}{figure.caption.255}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10dnoisetest}{{8.3}{210}{The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\,V, and it was exposed to 15\,mW of 1.55\,\si {\micro \metre } light. 15\,mA of photocurrent was generated. The noise of the photocurrent produced by the FD10D (blue line) corresponds to the level predicted by Equation~\ref {eq:pd_chapter_noise_budget} (orange line) meaning that the quantum efficiency of the FD10D did increase rather than the increase in photocurrent being the result of an avalanche process. The 1111\,Hz signal was used to calibrate the measurement as this modulation also could be measured on the high-performance in-loop photodiode. \relax }{figure.caption.255}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, and it was exposed to a series of different power levels to check at what photocurrent the photodiode would saturate at. This was done by measuring the height of the calibration peak; if this peak decreased in size relative to the DC signal then this would indicate the photodiode was saturating. This peak could only be measured to within 1\%, and so the FD10D showed no sign of saturation under these conditions.\relax }}{211}{figure.caption.256}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10dacdc}{{8.4}{211}{The bias of the FD10D was set to 0.6\,V, and it was exposed to a series of different power levels to check at what photocurrent the photodiode would saturate at. This was done by measuring the height of the calibration peak; if this peak decreased in size relative to the DC signal then this would indicate the photodiode was saturating. This peak could only be measured to within 1\%, and so the FD10D showed no sign of saturation under these conditions.\relax }{figure.caption.256}{}}
